  "isLocked": true,
  "description": "Filter redundant overlapping candidate bounding boxes iteratively retaining highest scoring detections.\n\nObject detectors propose hundreds of redundant overlapping anchor boxes around each target entity. NMS post-processing collapses overlaps to single confident bounding boxes.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\text{Keep } i, \\quad \\text{Suppress } j \\iff \\text{IoU}(B_i, B_j) > \\tau\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Sort candidate detections in descending order of classification scores\n2. Greedily retain highest-scoring bounding box\n3. Suppress all remaining boxes sharing IoU > iou_threshold with retained box\n\n### Returns\nList of integer indices of retained bounding boxes.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Non-Maximum Suppression\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nNeural networks achieve their remarkable expressive power by stacking linear layers interspersed with non-linear activation functions and normalization layers. Without non-linear activations, any deep network would collapse into a single shallow linear regression. **Non-Maximum Suppression** ensures signals propagate cleanly through dozens or hundreds of layers without vanishing or exploding.\n\nObject detectors propose hundreds of redundant overlapping anchor boxes around each target entity. NMS post-processing collapses overlaps to single confident bounding boxes. In this problem, the goal is to: filter redundant overlapping candidate bounding boxes iteratively retaining highest scoring detections.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Keep } i, \\quad \\text{Suppress } j \\iff \\text{IoU}(B_i, B_j) > \\tau\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Sort candidate detections in descending order of classification scores\n2. **Phase 2**: Greedily retain highest-scoring bounding box\n3. **Phase 3**: Suppress all remaining boxes sharing IoU > iou_threshold with retained box\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Gradient Flow**: Carefully designed activation functions (like ReLU, GELU, and Swish) maintain strong gradient signals during backpropagation.\n* **Internal Covariate Shift**: Normalization techniques (like LayerNorm and RMSNorm) standardize hidden states across channels or batch dimensions, allowing models to train stably at higher learning rates.\n* **Hardware Friendly**: Modern deep learning frameworks implement these layers using fused GPU kernels to reduce memory round-trips.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: boxes = [[0.0, 0.0, 2.0, 2.0], [0.1, 0.1, 2.1, 2.1], [5.0, 5.0, 7.0, 7.0]], scores = [0.9, 0.75, 0.85], iou_threshold = 0.5\n\nOutput: [0, 2]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated dl_nms under standard test conditions."
